From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Thu Sep 13 23:28:42 2018
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Thu, 13 Sep 2018 23:28:42 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] setting maxdist threshold in cleanGeno
Message-ID: <V-XyuycAtzU.vNqy5ogjBQAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <V-XyuycAtzU.ummexAbDBgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <V-XyuycAtzU.71xPN0TaBAAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com> <V-XyuycAtzU.ummexAbDBgAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/V-XyuycAtzU/m/vNqy5ogjBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you have low-density markers, it’s difficult to tell whether a particular genotype is an error. You can see that there are more double-crossovers then there should be, but you can’t tell which ones are errors.

If you have high-density markers, there will be more errors than you can carefully consider, individually.

But really, the things is that we’re not trying to determine the genotypes but rather to map QTL or to estimate a genetic map, and for those tasks it’s perfectly fine to just leave the errors in and use methods that are tolerant to the presence of errors.

karl

